.1093/ageing/27.5.569. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Rose DF. Preventing falls among older adults: no “one size suits all” intervention strategy. J Rehab Res Devel. 2008;45(8):1153–66. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supp Material NIHMS648487-supplement-Supp_Material.pdf (11.6KB, pdf)',1500,'2025-10-15 11:43:02','2025-10-15 11:43:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5952,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10132489','Fetal surgery is not associated with increased inflammatory placental pathology.','Cardenas MC, Cheek-Norgan EH, Branda ME, Norgan AP, Schenone MH, Lemens MA, Chakraborty R, Ruano R, Enninga EAL.','2023','ttts myelomeningocele cdh luto vesicoamniotic shunting cystoscopy superficially villitis villi villitis hofbauer microchimerism myelomeningocele villitis extraplacental procured procured extraplacental cdh luto hlhs ttts gravid villitis intervillous deciduitis malperfusion unprompted ripening placentae hemoxylin micron deparaffinization cd138 mi15 10d6 4b12 cd56 123c3 144b diaminobenzidine bx50 dp27 cellsens 200x cd56 cd138 graphed pprom endoluminal feto myelomeningocele gravidity chorioamnionitis abruption pprom tones dizygotic pprom cdh ttts luto hlhs feto septectomy cdh ttts luto hlhs feto fetoscopic endoluminal myelomeningocele 1889g dizygotic placentae placentae 409g 388g villi villous villitis intervillous deciduitis lymphoplasmacytic chorionitis decidual arteriopathy avascular villi hemosiderin villous villous chorionitis hemosiderin villitis intervillous lymphoplasmacytic deciduitis avascular villi villous decidual arteriopathy 200x 400x villitis 200x 400x villitis 200x malperfusion thrombosed avascular villi intervillous 200x chorioamnionitis cd56 cd138 syndecan cd56 cd138 hofbauer perivillous histiocytes faintly cd56 histiocytes perivillous cd138 villitis placentae cd56 cd138 histocytes placentae avascular villi hemosiderin haploidentical villitis histocytic intervillositis villitis villitis villitis villitis hofbauer cd138 syndecan cd138 cd138 malperfusion malperfusion villous villi villi pprom chorioamniotic fetoscopic placentae fetoscopic fetoscopic allogenic placentae ai151208 eale senat deprest boulvain paupe winer ville amnioreduction adzick thom spong myelomeningocele ruano klinkner balakrishnan fetoscopic deprest nicolaides benachi calster benachi nicolaides e561 e524 ruano sananes sangi haghpeykar cystoscopy vesicoamniotic shunting belfort shamshirsaz fetoscopic redline villitis villitis swieboda hofbauer hofbauer villitis frascoli coniglio alloreactive jeanty derderian saadai bautista intervillositis e13373 wegorzewska nijagal conceptus nijman benders jaiman bhatti derricott heazell villitis schmedt gotte heinig kiesel klockenbusch steinhard syndecan prenat egerman moorhead reseal borgida rodis moron reparative chorioamniotic myelomeningocele gratacos sanin lewi fetoscopic papanna moise fetoscopic sharhan fetoscopic prenat stelzer overrides altemani metze villitis langston khong ariel','Abstract Objective: Fetal surgery has improved neonatal outcomes; however, it is unknown if the intervention contributes to the developmental of inflammatory pathologies in the placenta. Here, an association between fetal surgery and placental pathology was examined. Method: This case-control study compared pregnancies with fetal surgery (n = 22), pregnancies with an indication for fetal surgery but without an intervention being done (n = 13), and gestational-age and fetus-number matched controls (n = 36). Data on maternal, infant, and placental outcomes was abstracted. Additionally, immunohistochemistry identified expression of lymphoid and myeloid cells in the placenta on a subset of cases. Comparisons were performed using Kruskal-Wallis or Pearson’s chi-squared tests. Results: Maternal characteristics were comparable between groups. Most fetal interventions were for diaphragmatic hernia, spina bifida, or twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome. Fetuses who were operated on before birth were more likely to be born preterm (p=0.02). There was no increase in the rate of observed placental pathologies or immune cell infiltration in fetal surgery cases compared to controls. Conclusion: The data suggest fetal surgery is not associated with increased inflammatory or morphologic pathology in the placenta. This observation supports the growing field of fetal surgery. Keywords: fetal surgery, placenta, pathology, inflammation, leukocyte, pregnancy Introduction Recent improvements in diagnostic technologies have led to the development of fetal surgery procedures designed to treat congenital anomalies in utero . Results to date have demonstrated improvement in outcomes of fetuses affected by twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) 1 , myelomeningocele (MMC) 2 , and congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) 3 – 5 . Intrauterine transfusion is often administered to fetuses diagnosed with anemia, while lower urinary tract obstruction (LUTO) has been managed by vesicoamniotic shunting and cystoscopy 6 . While clinical trials have demonstrated the efficacy of fetal surgery, these invasive procedures are associated with an increased risk of preterm delivery 2 – 4 , 6 , 7 . It remains unclear what leads to this, but immune perturbations in the mother-infant dyad could play a key role. The potential impact of in utero surgical interventions on systemic and placental inflammation has not been well characterized. Placental conditions associated with spontaneous preterm birth, at least superficially, resemble immunologic rejection. Chronic villitis is defined by infiltration of maternal cytotoxic T cells into the placenta villi, which they subsequently damage or destroy 8 . Chronic villitis can impair placental function and is associated with adverse outcomes such as preterm birth or stillbirth 8 . Placental macrophages, or Hofbauer cells (HCs), can induce strong inflammatory responses to infection and have an important, but undefined role in placental inflammation 9 , 10 . However, it is unknown whether or how HCs are impacted by surgical intervention. Systemic changes may also alter the delicate immune balance during pregnancy. Compared to term infants, cord blood from infants who were born preterm exhibited elevated maternal microchimerism (maternal cells in fetal cord blood) with elevated proinflammatory cytokines levels, and expansion of activated T cells associated with increased uterine contractility 11 . Although these observations were made in the setting of spontaneous preterm birth, there is evidence suggesting similar cellular mechanisms may also be involved in surgery-induced preterm birth. For example, increased levels of maternal cells in cord blood have been detected in fetuses that underwent myelomeningocele repair in utero compared to postnatal repair 12 , 13 . Whether fetal surgery contributes towards the development of immune-mediated placental pathology has not been fully addressed. The objective of this study was to determine whether surgical intervention leads to an increased incidence of villitis or other inflammatory pathologies of the placenta. The hypothesis was surgical intervention would result in an increase in placental inflammation by increased immune cell activation caused by surgical trauma, and that placental inflammation could contribute to the development of preterm labor. Methods This case-control study was approved by the Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board (#20–005377). Subjects were included into one of the following groups: 1) history of fetal surgery, 2) indication for, but no history of fetal surgery and, 3) controls ( Figure 1 ). Cases were identified through a retrospective medical record search, which consisted of pregnant women evaluated at Mayo Clinic from 2016 through February 2020 and who provided research authorization. Controls included non-anomalous pregnancies during this same timeframe. For all participants, women had to have delivered at Mayo Clinic, Rochester, or Mayo Clinic Health System, and have had pathological review of their placenta. Per standard placental review, each case had at least four unique blocks generated from the umbilical cord, extraplacental membranes, and parenchyma. Additional blocks were procured for parenchymal abnormalities identified macroscopically. The umbilical cord samples were procured just above the insertion point and at the mid aspect, the extraplacental membranes from rupture to insertion point, and the parenchyma from the central placenta near the umbilical cord insertion. Overall, each block represented an approximate 2.0 × 1.5 × 0.4 cm portion of sampled placental tissue. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Flow diagram of the overall study population and outcomes reported from each group. Surgical indications consisted of CDH, MMC, LUTO, hypoplastic left heart syndrome (HLHS) and TTTS. Controls cases were matched to fetal surgery cases by gestational age at delivery and the number of fetuses. This resulted in a total of 71 subjects: 22 who underwent fetal surgery, 13 having an indication for fetal surgery but not undergoing surgery, and 36 controls. Electronic medical review was used to collect data on maternal (age, ethnicity, gravid status, body mass index [BMI], gestational age at delivery, pregnancy complications and type of in utero surgery), fetal (intervention status, gestational age at delivery, birth weight, fetal sex, and Apgar scores at 1 and 5 minutes) and placental pathology variables (weight, villitis, intervillous thrombi, chronic deciduitis and fetal vascular malperfusion). Spontaneous delivery was defined as the unprompted commencement of labor (i.e., contractions, membrane rupture, cervical ripening). Study data were collected and managed using REDCap electronic data capture tools 14 , 15 . A total of 16 archived blocks from the initial cohort described above were available for additional analysis: 11 in the fetal surgery group (50%) and 5 from the normal control group (10%); no placentae from those fetuses with indication but no surgery was available. Previous diagnoses were confirmed for each case by hemoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining. Slides were cut at 5-micron tissue thickness followed by deparaffinization, citrate antigen retrieval and blocking. Tissue was stained in primary antibody (Agilent Dako, Santa Clara, CA) overnight for the following immune cell targets: CD138 (1:200; clone MI15), CD163 (1:400; clone 10D6), CD4 (1:100; clone 4B12), CD56 (1:700; clone 123C3) and CD8 (1:200; clone 144B). Slides were then incubated for 1 hour in HRP-conjugated secondary antibodies (Thermo Fisher, Waltham, MA), 15 minutes with 3,3′-Diaminobenzidine (DAB; Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis, MO) followed by hematoxylin. Positive and negative controls were reviewed to verify staining specificity. H&E and immunohistochemical stained slides were reviewed utilizing a BX50 Olympus microscope (Olympus Corporation Tokyo, Japan). Digital images were captured using a DP27 camera attached to the microscope and CellSens Standard software (Olympus Corporation Tokyo, Japan). Semi-quantitative measurements of DAB staining intensity were made using ImageJ, following a previously described protocol 16 . Five representative images from each case were captured at 200X optical magnification. To calculate DAB intensity, images from each case were measured at the following thresholds: 128 for CD4, 144 for CD8, 140 for CD56, 146 for CD138 and 138 for CD163. Group statistics for the continuous variables were expressed as mean +/− standard deviation (SD) or median with interquartile range (IQR), depending on the normality of the distribution. Comparison of variables between groups was performed using a Kruskal-Wallis test. Nominal variables were summarized as frequencies and percentages and compared using Pearson’s chi-squared test. For IHC staining comparisons, the fetal surgery and control groups were compared using the Mann-Whitney test. Statistical analyses were performed using SAS (version 9.4) and graphed using GraphPad Prism 9.1 (GraphPad Software, San Diego, CA), with 2-sided p <0.05 considered statistically significant. Results The matching strategy utilized resulted in no significant differences in maternal baseline characteristics between the fetal surgery, indication for surgery but no intervention and control pregnancies ( Table 1 ). Compared to the non-surgical intervention group, who were not matched by gestational age, delivery was significantly earlier in the fetal surgery group (31 versus 35 weeks, p=0.02). This difference was predominately due to an increase in premature preterm rupture of membranes (PPROM) in the surgery cases compared to those that did not have an intervention (45% vs. 15%, p=0.07). When comparing indication for delivery, there were no differences based on whether delivery was spontaneous or based on maternal/fetal causes between groups. Overall, 64% of the preterm control cases spontaneously delivered, which was equal to spontaneous deliveries in the preterm fetal surgery (61%) and preterm non-surgical intervention (60%) groups. There was a similar distribution of ten fetal indications for procedures between the surgical and non-surgical groups (p=0.63; Table 2 ). In the surgical group, approximately 68% of the fetuses underwent surgery before 28 weeks, with 27% having surgery between 28–31 6/7 weeks. Over 75% of the surgery cases (17/22) had a minimally invasive rather than an open procedure. Laser ablation was the most common procedure performed (27%), followed by fetal endoluminal tracheal occlusion (FETO) and open myelomeningocele (MMC) repair (22% each). Four out of the 22 fetuses had a second in utero intervention to place a shunt or sample the umbilical vein before 28 weeks gestation. Table 1: Maternal demographics and obstetrical outcomes Fetal surgery (N=22) Control (N=36) No fetal surgery (N=13) P-Value (Surgery vs. Control) P-Value (Surgery vs. No Surgery) Maternal Age : Mean (SD) 30.2 (5.05) 28.4 (5.29) 27.0 (5.18) 0.23 1 0.08 1 Race 0.61 2 0.86 2 American Indian/Alaska Native 1 (5%) 1 (3%) 0 (0%) Asian 2 (9%) 4 (11%) 1 (8%) Black or African American 1 (5%) 0 (0%) 1 (8%) White 18 (82%) 31 (86%) 11 (85%) Smoking Status 0.48 2 0.77 2 Never 16 (76%) 22 (61%) 10 (77%) Quit 3 (14%) 7 (19%) 1 (8%) Current 2 (10%) 7 (19%) 2 (15%) Missing 1 0 0 Chronic Conditions 0