1%) 1 (7.7%) Spina Bifida - MMC 5 (22.7%) 2 (15.4%) HLHS 1 (4.5%) 0 (0%) CHAOS 1 (4.5%) 0 (0%) Fetal Anemia 2 (9.1%) 0 (0%) Fetal Ascites 1 (4.5%) 0 (0%) TRAP 1 (4.5%) 0 (0%) Gestational Age @ Surgery (weeks), n (%) <28 weeks 15 (68.2%) ~ 28 – 31 6/7 6 (27.3%) ~ 32 – 35 6/7 1 (4.5%) ~ Intervention Type , n (%) Laser ablation 6 (27.3%) ~ PUBS 3 (13.6%) ~ FETO 5 (22.7%) ~ Shunt Placement 2 (9.1%) ~ Open MMC Repair 5 (22.7%) ~ Balloon atrial septectomy 1 (4.5%) ~ Second Intervention , n (%) No 18 (81.8%) ~ Yes 4 (18.2%) ~ Type of Second Intervention , n (%) PUBS 2 (50.0%) ~ Shunt Placement 2 (50.0%) ~ Gestational Age at 2 nd Intervention , n (%) <28 weeks 3 (75.0%) ~ 28 – 31 6/7 1 (25.0%) Open in a new tab 1 Chi-Square p-value; Abbreviations: CDH = congenital diaphragmatic hernia; TTTS = twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome; IUGR = intrauterine growth restriction; LUTO = lower urinary tract obstruction; HLHS = hypoplastic left heart syndrome; CHAOS = congenital high airway obstruction syndrome; TRAP = twin reversed arterial perfusion; PUBS = percutaneous umbilical blood sampling; FETO = fetoscopic endoluminal tracheal occlusion; MMC = myelomeningocele Table 3 describes the neonatal outcomes of the cohort. Due to inclusion of multiples, 8 sets of twins were included in the analysis, with one twin set in the fetal surgery group including only one neonate due to an early fetal demise of the other. There were more male fetuses than females in all groups, although the distribution was similar across cohorts. Matching by gestational age at delivery controls for differences in placental features based on trimester, which means the control cohort intentionally included premature infants. No differences in birth weight were noted between fetal surgery and controls (2577 vs. 1889g, p=0.06). Newborns in the surgery group had significantly worse 1- and 5-minute Apgar scores (p=0.001) compared to the controls, but there were no differences when comparing to the group without fetal surgery (p=0.22) despite a significant skew in gestational age at birth between these two groups. The majority (85%) of neonates in this cohort were admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit and spent an average of 43.5 days (range 35.6 – 49.5 days) in the hospital. Table 3: Neonatal outcomes Fetal surgery (N=27) 3 Control (N=40) No fetal surgery (N=16) P-Value (Surgery vs. Control) P-Value (Surgery vs. No Surgery) Fetal Sex , n (%) 0.70 1 0.47 1 Female 11 (42.3%) 15 (37.5%) 5 (31.3%) Male 15 (57.7%) 25 (62.5%) 11 (68.8%) Birth weight (g) , Mean (SD) 1889 (1202) 2053 (1004) 2576.7 (1185) 0.60 2 0.06 2 Missing 1 0 2 Apgar - 1 minute 0.002 1 0.43 1 < 8 22 (84.6%) 17 (42.5%) 12 (75.0%) ≥ 8 4 (15.4%) 23 (57.5%) 4 (25.0%) Apgar - 5 minutes 0.001 1 0.29 1 < 8 17 (65.4%) 8 (20.0%) 8 (50.0%) ≥ 8 9 (34.6%) 32 (80.0%) 8 (50.0%) NICU Admission 0.15 1 0.22 1 No 4 (15.4%) 2 (5.0%) 5 (31.3%) Yes 22 (84.6%) 38 (95.0%) 11 (68.8%) Length of time in NICU (days), Mean (SD) 49.5 (62.4) 35.6 (41.7) 45.5 (83.2) 0.17 2 0.19 2 Median (IQR), (Range) 40.5 (13, 52), (1 – 296) 18 (5, 52), (1 – 154) 15 (9, 34), (1 – 290) Open in a new tab 1 Chi-Square p-value 2 Kruskal-Wallis p-value 3 One fetus that is a product of multiples in the Fetal Surgery group was a demise before birth and all values are missing from the table for this case. Abbreviations: NICU = neonatal intensive care unit Pathology reports were compared between groups to determine whether fetal surgery increases the presence of certain histologic findings, controlled for by gestational age. As this cohort included twins (i.e., dizygotic), results were analyzed from 24 placentae that had fetal surgery, 15 that had an indication but did not undergo surgery and 40 controls. The average weight of placentae was 409g in the fetal surgery cohort, 483.7g in the no fetal surgery cohort and 388g in the control group ( Table 4 ), which was not significantly different. The placental villi were appropriately mature for gestational age in most cases, with no differences in villous maturation between study groups ( Figure 2 ). There were no significant differences or trends in the frequency of diagnosis of chronic villitis, intervillous thrombi, deciduitis (both chronic or lymphoplasmacytic), infarcts, acute chorionitis, acute fetal vasculitis, single umbilical artery, edema or decidual arteriopathy between groups. The fetal surgery group had a significant increase in the presence of avascular villi and membrane-associated hemosiderin-laden macrophages (p=0.02). Together, these results suggest that fetal surgery does not result in increased abnormal morphologic findings by placental evaluation. Table 4: Summary of placental pathology findings Fetal surgery (N=24) Control (N=40) No fetal surgery (N=15) P-Value (Surgery vs. Control) P-Value (Surgery vs. No Surgery) Placenta Weight (g), Mean (SD) 409.0 (153.71) 388.0 (120.76) 483.7 (189.92) 0.35 1 0.13 1 Placenta Weight for GA (g), n (%) 0.84 2 0.98 2 Small < 10%ile 7 (29%) 10 (25%) 4 (27%) Appropriate for GA 9 (38%) 18 (45%) 6 (40%) Large >90%ile 8 (33%) 12 (30%) 5 (33%) Placenta size , n (%) 0.87 2 0.61 2 Appropriate for GA 18 (75%) 32 (80%) 9 (60%) Delayed villous maturation 1 (4%) 1 (3%) 1 (7%) Accelerated villous maturation 5 (21%) 7 (18%) 5 (33%) Pathologic Findings , n (%) Single Umbilical Artery 2 (8%) 0 1 (17%) 0.06 2 0.85 2 Acute Chorionitis 2 (8%) 8 (20%) 3 (20%) 0.21 2 0.29 2 Hemosiderin-laden macrophages 3 (13%) 0 0 0.02 2 0.15 2 Chronic villitis 3 (13%) 3 (8%) 1 (7%) 0.51 2 0.56 2 Intervillous thrombi 6 (25%) 15 (38%) 3 (20%) 0.30 2 0.72 2 Chronic or lymphoplasmacytic deciduitis 0 (0%) 3 (8%) 1 (7%) 0.17 2 0.20 2 Infarct 4 (17%) 7 (18%) 3 (20%) 0.93 2 0.79 2 Avascular villi 3 (13%) 0 (0%) 0 (0%) 0.02 2 0.15 2 Acute fetal vasculitis 2 (8%) 4 (10%) 1 (7%) 0.82 2 0.85 2 Villous edema 3 (13%) 1 (3%) 0 (0%) 0.11 2 0.15 2 Decidual arteriopathy 1 (4%) 4 (10%) 0 (0%) 0.40 2 0.42 2 Open in a new tab 1 Kruskal-Wallis p-value 2 Chi-Square p-value; GA = gestational age Figure 2: Representative images of pathologies commonly encountered in both the control and fetal intervention cohorts. Open in a new tab A) Intervention (200x, H&E) and control (400x, H&E) demonstrate low-grade chronic villitis; B) Surgery (200x, H&E) and control (400x, H&E) show basal chronic villitis; C) Intervention and control (200x, H&E) display features of fetal vascular malperfusion, with a thrombosed stem vessel and avascular villi, respectively; D) Intervention (100x, H&E) with an intervillous thrombus and control (200x, acute chorioamnionitis), which were found in both cohorts. Whether changes in placental immune cell populations were associated with in utero intervention was addressed by additional IHC staining of available placental blocks. Photomicrographs were compared between groups for CD4, CD8, CD56, CD163 and CD138/syndecan-1 ( Figure 3 ). By manual review there were no detectable changes in immune cell infiltration between the control and fetal surgery group. Subsequently, DAB staining intensity was quantified using ImageJ software ( Figure 4 ). There was no observed increase in CD4+ T helper cell intensity in the fetal surgery cases compared to controls (2.0 vs. 12.4 pixels, respectively; p=0.15). CD8+ cytotoxic T cells were also not significantly different between surgical intervention cases and controls (0.4 vs. 3.8 pixels; p=0.15). Virtually no CD56+ NK cell staining (0.1 vs. 0.2 pixels; p=0.23) were observed, and there was similar CD138+ expression (40.28 vs. 16.25 pixels; p=0.28) between surgical intervention cases and controls. Lastly, CD163+ fetal Hofbauer cells showed no differences in DAB intensity in controls and surgical intervention cases (13.1 vs. 16.4 pixels; p=0.68). No demonstrable increase in immune cell infiltration into the placenta was evident at the time of delivery. Figure 3: Representative images of immunohistochemical staining of immune cells in the intervention and control cohorts, demonstrating similar staining patterns. Open in a new tab Row 1) H&E stain displaying lymphocytic infiltrate with patchy perivillous fibrin; Row 2) CD4+ T-cells, histiocytes, or monocytes are faintly positive; Row 3) CD8+ T cytotoxic cells are strongly positive; Row 4) CD56+ NK cells show absent staining; Row 5) CD163+ histiocytes are strongly positive, with areas of perivillous fibrin also staining positive; Row 6) CD138+ plasma cells and trophoblast cell membranes are strongly positive in the trophoblastic lining, but negative in focus of chronic villitis. Figure 4: Quantification of immune cell staining intensity in placentae from fetal surgery and control cohorts. Open in a new tab Median pixel intensity between groups for A) CD4+ helper T cells; B) CD8+ cytotoxic T cells; C) CD56+ NK cells; D) CD138+ plasma and trophoblast cells and E) CD163+ histocytes. Results displayed as medians with interquartile ranges (n=5–11/group). Significance determined by Mann-Whitney testing. Discussion Using a cohort of placentae from fetuses diagnosed with a congenital anomaly who had and did not have fetal surgery, compared to gestational age-matched controls, the results did not demonstrate increased inflammation or diagnosis of pathologic abnormalities in the placenta. There was a significant increase in avascular villi and hemosiderin-laden macrophages identified in the fetal surgery group. While it is conceivable that either of these features could be attributable to the surgical invention, the presence of the findings in only 3 (of 24) cases, does not suggest a strong association. These data suggest included fetal procedures were not associated with more placental abnormalities evident by histology at the time of delivery. As certain placental conditions are associated with a maternal rejection response to the haploidentical fetus 8 , 17 , which may be prompted by mixing of fetal and maternal cells during in utero surgery 18 , an increase in chronic villitis and chronic histocytic intervillositis (CHI) diagnoses may occur. Instead, no CHI and only three cases of chronic villitis were present in the fetal surgery group, which was not significantly more compared to those that did not undergo surgery 19 , 20 . When immune cell phenotypes were examined by IHC, two cases in the control group and one in the surgery group had a chronic villitis diagnosis, which accounts for the outliers noted on pixel density quantification. Chronic villitis pathophysiology is mainly driven by CD8+ T cells, but CD4+ cells have also been noted during this etiology 8 , 21 . Additionally, chronic villitis is associated with an increase in Hofbauer cells 10 , which was seen by elevated CD163+ staining in individual cases from both groups. CD138, also known as syndecan-1, is expressed on trophoblast cells and increased expression of CD138 has been linked to preterm birth and growth 22 . No difference in CD138 expression was observed between surgical and control cases, suggesting that this molecule is not part of the pathophysiology of preterm birth after fetal surgery. Studies examining histology of the spontaneously delivered preterm placenta have described a switch from the diagnosis of infectious or inflammatory lesions to maternal vascular malperfusion after 28 weeks gestation 19 . Another group also found an association with spontaneous preterm birth and maternal vascular malperfusion, as well abnormal villous maturation 20 . Of those in the current study who underwent fetal surgery, 75% had villi that were appropriate for gestational age, and those who had abnormal villi were more likely to exhibit accelerated maturation compared to delayed. Pregnancies in the surgery cohort were significantly more complicated by PPROM, which was the main reason for premature birth. Physiologic repair of damaged fetal membranes has been described both in spontaneous rupture and rupture after invasive procedures 23 , 24 . Yet, mechanisms that promote repair remain unclear. Studies evaluating membranes following open fetal surgery found that collagen synthesis was significantly increased around the suture site of the chorioamniotic membranes compared to non-surgical areas 25 . Other groups found no indication of membrane healing following fetoscopic procedures 26 , 27 . Information regarding the impact of fetal surgery on the placenta parenchyma is scarce. In placentae from MMC repair cases compared by fetoscopic or open procedure, there were no significant differences in histopathology based on surgical approach, except for fewer pigment laden macrophages in the fetoscopic cohort 28 . Similar to the results presented here, there were no changes in diagnosis of inflammatory lesions following fetal surgery. Future studies that link systemic immunity with the placental pathology will be useful for determining an association with systemic maternal or fetal activation 13 , 18 . However, counter immunomodulatory measures at the maternal-fetal interface that require further characterization may suppress these affects locally. It will also be important to address the impact of fetal surgery on future pregnancies and placental pathology. Additionally, first and subsequent pregnancies utilize different mechanisms to support the development of a fetus 29 ; therefore, if additional priming of the immune system to the allogenic fetus occurs with surgery, subsequent pregnancies may be at higher risk of complications. These clinically and biologically important questions remain, but their answers could lead to improved neonatal outcomes in subsequent pregnancies. This study is not without limitations. First, only a small portion of a placenta is sampled for pathological evaluation and therefore diagnoses could be missed. 30 For all placental evaluations, our organization utilizes the guidelines developed by the College of American Pathologists 31 , and the Amsterdam Working Group for standardization of reporting and to minimize missed diagnoses 32 . Additionally, as fetal surgery remains a rare occurrence, analysis based on diagnosis and type of surgery performed could not be completed. Connecting clinical outcomes with pathologic findings remains challenging because tertiary centers having surgeons with the expertise to offer fetal interventions are limited, therefore patients seeking this option often travel for the surgery and deliver their infant locally. While the current study describes a moderate sized cohort of placental outcomes following surgery at a single institution, it will be important for specialized fetal centers to work collaboratively to increase the power of these associations. These collaborations could further support the effectiveness and safety of fetal surgery and help to address which mechanisms are promoting the increase incidence of preterm labor in this group. Conclusion In conclusion, this study demonstrated that fetal surgery is not associated with an increased incidence of placental pathology. This study provides modest reassurance to physicians, review boards, and patients considering in utero intervention, that fetal surgery likely does not significantly impact placental histology and function. What’s already known about this topic? Fetal surgery improves clinical outcomes caused by some congenital anomalies; however, it is unclear whether these surgeries are associated with distinct placental pathologies What does this study add? Fetal surgery was not associated with additional placental pathologies or immune cell infiltration compared to placentae from neonates with a congenital anomaly who did not undergo surgery or gestational age-matched controls Funding Statement: Support for this project was provided by the National Institute of Health AI151208 to RR and EALE. The funding source was not involved in this research. Footnotes Conflict of Interest: The authors report no conflicts of interest. Data availability: Deidentified information on the cases and controls presented here are available from the corresponding author upon request. References 1. Senat MV, Deprest J, Boulvain M, Paupe A, Winer N, Ville Y. Endoscopic laser surgery versus serial amnioreduction for severe twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome. N Engl J Med. 2004;351(2):136–144. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Adzick NS, Thom EA, Spong CY, et al. A randomized trial of prenatal versus postnatal repair of myelomeningocele. N Engl J Med. 2011;364(11):993–1004. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ruano R, Klinkner DB, Balakrishnan K, et al. Fetoscopic Therapy for Severe Pulmonary Hypoplasia in Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia: A First in Prenatal Regenerative Medicine at Mayo Clinic. Mayo Clin Proc. 2018;93(6):693–700. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Deprest JA, Nicolaides KH, Benachi A, et al. Randomized Trial of Fetal Surgery for Severe Left Diaphragmatic Hernia. N Engl J Med. 2021. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Van Calster B, Benachi A, Nicolaides KH, et al. The randomized Tracheal Occlusion To Accelerate Lung growth (TOTAL)-trials on fetal surgery for congenital diaphragmatic hernia: reanalysis using pooled data. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2022;226(4):560.e561–560.e524. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ruano R, Sananes N, Sangi-Haghpeykar H, et al. Fetal intervention for severe lower urinary tract obstruction: a multicenter case-control study comparing fetal cystoscopy with vesicoamniotic shunting. Ultrasound Obstet Gynecol. 2015;45(4):452–458. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Belfort MA, Whitehead WE, Shamshirsaz AA, et al. Fetoscopic Open Neural Tube Defect Repair: Development and Refinement of a Two-Port, Carbon Dioxide Insufflation Technique. Obstet Gynecol. 2017;129(4):734–743. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Redline RW. Villitis of unknown etiology: noninfectious chronic villitis in the placenta. Hum Pathol. 2007;38(10):1439–1446. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Swieboda D, Johnson EL, Beaver J, 